<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rímac River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rímac is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s central coast that flows from the Andes through the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> to the Pacific at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>. Its valley held the Maranga complex of the Lima culture and later shrines of the Ychsma, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> founded Lima on its south bank in 1535. Its name, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> for 'speaker', is linked to an oracle in the valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rimac-river.jpg" alt="Rímac River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ministerio de Defensa del Perú · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MINISTRO_DE_DEFENSA_SOBREVOL%C3%93_LIMA_PARA_SUPERVISAR_ACATAMIENTO_DE_AISLAMIENTO_SOCIAL_OBLIGATORIO.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Rímac begins on the western slope of the Andes at about 5,508 metres above sea level, on the Nevado Paca, in the highlands of Huarochirí Province. It runs through the provinces of Huarochirí and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and reaches the sea at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>, near Jorge Chávez International Airport. The river is 204 km long and drains a basin of 3,312 km². Its main tributaries are the Santa Eulalia, the San Mateo or Alto Rímac and the Blanco. Like the Chillón to the north and the Lurín to the south, it belongs to the Pacific slope.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> stands on the delta-shaped lower valley, one of the city's three valleys. Towns such as Matucana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ricardo-palma">Ricardo Palma</a>, Chosica, Chaclacayo and Vitarte lie along it and link the coast with the central mountains. The Central Highway and a railway run beside the river from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> to La Oroya, passing the Abra de Anticona, known as Ticlio, at 4,840 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> word rimaq, 'speaker', and the river was nicknamed El Río Hablador, 'the talking river'. One explanation is that people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> area built galleries at the river's edge with a space where a priest could enter unseen. The inhabitants approached the river, which they regarded as an apu, or deity, and put their questions to it, and the priest answered from inside. A legend instead derives the name from the sacrifice of Rímac, a son of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, and his sister Chaclla, to end a drought.</p><p>The Spanish misheard Rímac as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, and the city took its name from the river. The form Limaq seems to have been the original in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and in the Mochica language that preceded it. The best known of the bridges at Lima is the Puente de Piedra, built in 1610 under the viceroy Juan de Mendoza y Luna, Marquis of Montesclaros.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Rímac is the main source of drinking water for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> metropolitan area. The largest discharge recorded at the Chosica station was 385 cubic metres a second, in 1941. In 1962 water was brought into the basin from the Marcapomacocha lagoon, in the Mantaro basin, through a siphon-shaped tunnel about 10 km long at 4,000 metres. The basin holds a water treatment plant, La Atarjea, and several hydroelectric plants. In 1998 a reserved zone of the river valley was created along a 28 km strip between Lima, Chaclacayo, San Juan de Lurigancho and Ate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rímac River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lima culture took shape around 100 CE in the Chillón, <b>Rímac</b> and Lurín valleys of Peru's central coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/lima-culture">Lima culture</a></li><li><blockquote>Ychsma, or Ichma, emerged around 1100 in the Lurín and lower <b>Rímac</b> valleys, where modern Lima stands, after the decline of Wari influence.</blockquote><a href="/state/ychsma">Ychsma</a></li></ul></section>
